---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { docs, layers, plannerStages, providers, sourceUrl } from "../../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout title="Architecture — OrbitKV" description="Current KV cache architecture and the planned distributed catalog and router." active="Architecture">
  <div class="container interior">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
      <h1>Local first.<br /><span class="highlight">Distributed next.</span></h1>
      <p>vLLM and SGLang own GPU pages and execution. OrbitKV owns external cache replicas and transfer leases. Remote-cache recovery comes before KV-aware routing.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="current-title">
      <h2 id="current-title">What works today</h2>
      <p class="reading-copy">Each inference process connects to a Cache Manager on its own host. UDS and iceoryx2 carry lifecycle and cache commands; CUDA IPC exposes engine-owned GPU pages. The manager stores reusable KV in pinned DRAM and optional SSD. Versioned queries and byte budgets cover preparation, leased results, and GPU consumers. Both adapters passed single-rank DRAM and forced-SSD recovery gates, with shared/mixed bursts measured at concurrency 1, 4, and 8. Multi-rank serving and sustained goodput need separate qualification.</p>
      <p class="reading-copy">Distributed Managers now host fixed catalog shards and resolve them through cached etcd membership. Peer gRPC carries inventory and source authorization; Mooncake moves KV bytes. Per-shard snapshots and deltas repair lost evidence. Positive candidate caching and source runtime/residency checks are implemented. Each shard currently has one metadata copy, so HA and multi-host transfer qualification remain unfinished.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="crates-title">
      <h2 id="crates-title">Code boundaries</h2>
      <div class="definition-list">
        {layers.map((layer) => (<div><a class="package-name" href={sourceUrl(layer.path)}>{layer.name} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><p>{layer.detail}</p></div>))}
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="compile-title">
      <h2 id="compile-title">Target: a validated state plan</h2>
      <ol class="compile-flow">{plannerStages.map((stage) => (<li><h3>{stage.name}</h3><p>{stage.detail}</p></li>))}</ol>
      <p class="reading-copy">The current hot path uses versioned model and storage identities with native prefix hashes, but still carries raw GPU page IDs. The common state types do not yet prove complete token coverage or a restorable bundle. The proposed planner combines legal recovery boundaries, declared request demand, and measured transfer cost. The full cost-based planner remains unimplemented. Owner-inventory recovery is implemented; distributed serving qualification comes before routing.</p>
      <dl class="provider-list">{providers.map((provider) => (<div><dt>{provider.name}</dt><dd>{provider.role}</dd></div>))}</dl>
      <p class="reading-copy">Embedded catalogs, bounded candidate caches, and etcd member registration are implemented. A committed host set assigns 16 fixed shards without remapping on temporary member loss; block lookups stay off etcd. The next stages add replicated placement, controlled handoff and subscriptions. A later router may consume catalog evidence without entering cache transfer.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href={sourceUrl("docs/distributed-cache.md")}>Distributed cache plan and implementation stages <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </section>
    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="docs-title">
      <h2 id="docs-title">Read the contracts</h2>
      <div class="doc-links">{docs.map((doc) => (<a href={sourceUrl(doc.path)}>{doc.name}<span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>))}</div>
    </section>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
